WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.018
How long can you keep a phone number in your head if you do not repeat it? Short-term memory, or STM, is the store that holds a small amount of information for a short time.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:37.335
In nineteen fifty-nine, Peterson and Peterson tested this with twenty-four psychology students. They heard a trigram, which is three consonants such as C H J. Then a three-digit number. They counted backwards from that number in threes or fours. This stopped rehearsal, which means repeating information to keep it. After a delay, a light told them to recall the trigram.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:54.540
The delays were three, six, nine, twelve, fifteen and eighteen seconds. After three seconds, recall was about eighty to ninety per cent. After eighteen seconds it was under ten per cent. So without rehearsal, STM fades in about eighteen seconds.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:13.113
A further experiment found that saying items aloud, called vocal rehearsal, gave better recall than silent rehearsal. A weakness is that trigrams are artificial, so the study lacks ecological validity. That means it does not match real-life memory.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:21.520
A question may ask for the procedure, a finding, or a weakness. Or it may describe a person and ask you to apply the finding.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:40.372
Step one: recall the procedure in order: trigram, three-digit number, count back, light, recall. Step two: state the key finding. Step three: link it to the person. Step four: for a weakness, compare the task with real life.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:52.268
Two marks usually means a finding and its link. The traps are saying participants learned words or numbers, and forgetting that counting backwards stopped rehearsal.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:15.572
Here is a real exam question. On Saturday Chelsea silently repeated ten items on the way to the shop. On Sunday she said ten different items out loud. She remembered more on Sunday. We must explain why, using Peterson and Peterson. Two marks.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:24.081
Step one: find what is different. Only the way she repeated the items. Saturday silent, Sunday out loud.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:31.831
Step two: the finding. Peterson and Peterson found vocal rehearsal gave better recall than silent rehearsal.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:42.976
Step three: link it. Sunday was out loud, like vocal rehearsal. Saturday was silent. So she recalled more on Sunday.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:56.336
A tempting wrong route is to say the Sunday items were easier, or she was less tired. The stem says nothing like that. Reject it. Use what the stem gives.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:03.349
The exam answer. State the finding. Then say what Chelsea did on each day.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:13.081
One mark for the finding. One mark for linking it to Chelsea's two days. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:29.712
A common mistake is to get the procedure wrong. Students say participants learned words and recalled whenever they liked. In fact they heard a trigram, counted back, and recalled after three to eighteen seconds.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:42.909
Now you try. State two parts of the procedure used in Peterson and Peterson. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:04:01.382
Any two parts of the procedure earn the marks. They heard a trigram, three consonants. They counted backwards in threes or fours, which stopped rehearsal. A light told them when to recall, after three to eighteen seconds.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:16.393
The most common wrong answer says they learned words or numbers. The stimulus was a trigram. Another loses the mark by saying they counted to stop forgetting. The counting stopped rehearsal.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:24.895
So, recall the procedure in order. State the finding. Then link it to the person, or to real life.
